              "label": "Polarised dust · December 2024",
              "thumbnail": "betelgeuse-lens-matisse.webp",
              "volume": {
                "objectId": "betelgeuse-shell",
                "lensId": "zimpol-v",
                "surface": "matisse"
              },
              "notes": "The photosphere as the MATISSE reconstruction shows it, with the dust around it: the degree of linear polarisation VLT/SPHERE-ZIMPOL measured in the V band on 3 December 2024, released by ESO as Phase 3 collection BETELGEUSE-B beside Montargès et al. (2026). The patches one to about 4.5 stellar radii out are starlight scattered by dust. This is their own Fig. B.1 given depth: the same inferno colour map on the same zero-to-0.10 scale, carried as four emission channels so a line of sight emits the bar colour of its own measured degree. The colours are a legend for a ratio, not the colour of the dust. The instrumental polarisation measured beyond eight radii is subtracted, so the empty sky is darker here than in the printed figure. The map is a sky-plane image with no third axis, so it is placed in the plane of the sky through the star and spread along the line of sight by the scattering-angle efficiency of polarised light; that depth is a convention, not a measurement.",
